WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:02.413
The tree parser does three jobs in one

2
00:00:02.413 --> 00:00:04.195
45-line function.

3
00:00:04.195 --> 00:00:05.358
We'll pull them apart,

4
00:00:05.358 --> 00:00:07.800
one small edit at a time.

5
00:00:08.270 --> 00:00:10.537
First, the scan loop turns each line into

6
00:00:10.537 --> 00:00:12.770
a depth, a name and a note.

7
00:00:12.770 --> 00:00:15.375
It also guesses the indent unit.

8
00:00:15.545 --> 00:00:18.350
Second, the rebase shifts depths so the shallowest

9
00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:20.025
entry is zero.

10
00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:22.663
Third, the second loop decides which entries are

11
00:00:22.663 --> 00:00:26.150
folders, repairs bad indents and builds ids

12
00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:27.925
from the parent path.

13
00:00:28.145 --> 00:00:30.900
Last, a dedupe pass appends tildes to repeated

14
00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:32.300
ids.

15
00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:35.207
The first loop has two small knots.

16
00:00:35.207 --> 00:00:37.337
The note handling tests for a hash and

17
00:00:37.337 --> 00:00:40.100
then partitions, but partition already copes with a

18
00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:41.395
missing separator.

19
00:00:41.395 --> 00:00:43.525
So one line does it.

20
00:00:43.645 --> 00:00:46.170
The indent unit is set only once.

21
00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:48.475
Writing it as an or expression gives the

22
00:00:48.475 --> 00:00:50.720
same result with half the lines.

23
00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:52.620
Rows also become tuples,

24
00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:55.000
since nothing mutates them yet.

25
00:00:55.145 --> 00:00:57.500
That frees the rebase step to be absorbed

26
00:00:57.500 --> 00:00:58.900
elsewhere.

27
00:00:59.345 --> 00:01:01.345
The tail has three passes.

28
00:01:01.345 --> 00:01:04.113
The rebase mutates every row just to subtract

29
00:01:04.113 --> 00:01:05.058
the minimum.

30
00:01:05.058 --> 00:01:08.500
The dedupe walks every item a second time.

31
00:01:08.645 --> 00:01:10.620
Both fold into the main loop.

32
00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:13.300
The base is computed once and subtracted as

33
00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:14.333
we go.

34
00:01:14.333 --> 00:01:17.138
The dedupe becomes a local id checked against

35
00:01:17.138 --> 00:01:19.800
a set before the item is built.

36
00:01:19.945 --> 00:01:21.420
Behaviour is the same,

37
00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:23.525
with two fewer loops and no mutation of

38
00:01:23.525 --> 00:01:24.800
rows.

39
00:01:25.245 --> 00:01:26.770
Two passes are gone,

40
00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:29.433
and the note and indent-unit logic shrank.

41
00:01:29.433 --> 00:01:31.488
The output is the same dicts and the

42
00:01:31.488 --> 00:01:33.675
same problem messages.

43
00:01:33.845 --> 00:01:35.495
Next step, if you want it:

44
00:01:35.495 --> 00:01:38.070
move the depth rules into their own helper,

45
00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:40.945
so the scan loop only reads lines.

46
00:01:40.945 --> 00:01:43.920
Run the existing tree tests before and after,

47
00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:46.937
since the dedupe order and the indent-error messages

48
00:01:46.937 --> 00:01:49.450
are the parts most likely to slip.
